---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { API } from '../lib/api';
import { getAuthors, getPublishers, getTitles } from '../lib/catalogue';

const [publishers, authors, titles] = await Promise.all([getPublishers(), getAuthors(), getTitles()]);
const verified = titles.filter((t) => t.verified).length;
const n = (x: number) => x.toLocaleString('en-GB');
// The download's choice of one publisher, by name.
const sortedPublishers = [...publishers].sort((a, b) => a.data.name.localeCompare(b.data.name));
---

<Base centered title="About" description="About Bibliotheca Marxista, an open catalogue of Leftist and Marxist publishers' backlists: what it covers, where its records come from, and how to download the data.">
  <h1>About Bibliotheca Marxista</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    The printed output of the twentieth-century Left — book clubs, party presses, state
    publishers of translated classics — is scattered across library catalogues, dealer lists
    and personal shelves. This project gathers those backlists in one open, citable place.
  </p>

  <div class="stats">
    <div><strong>{n(publishers.length)}</strong><span>publishers</span></div>
    <div><strong>{n(titles.length)}</strong><span>titles</span></div>
    <div><strong>{n(verified)}</strong><span>verified against a library record</span></div>
    <div><strong>{n(authors.length)}</strong><span>author pages</span></div>
  </div>

  <h2>What counts</h2>
  <p>
    Any publishing house, press, imprint or book club whose list was substantially Marxist,
    communist, socialist or anti-colonial — party and state presses, independent radical houses and
    book clubs, defunct or still active, from any country and in any language. Where a house later
    turned to a general list, only its years as a press of the Left are catalogued. The globe on the home page places each press by country and
    by its side in the Cold War; the <a href="/international">International</a> pages group presses
    by the Internationals and movements they belonged to.
  </p>

  <h2>Authors</h2>
  <p>
    Author pages cover Marxist, socialist and anti-colonial writers, theorists and organisers —
    party leaders and historians, novelists and poets of the Left, guerrilla memoirists, and
    Marxist and socialist feminists who wrote on gender, patriarchy and social reproduction —
    from every continent. Each page lists the author's editions in the catalogue, together with
    the first editions of their main works, and cites a biographical source.
  </p>

  <h2>Verified and unverified records</h2>
  <p>
    A title is marked <span class="badge verified">verified</span> when it has been matched to a
    library catalogue record — from a national or research library such as those listed below, or
    an Open Library record copied from one — whose imprint names this publisher, its place and a
    year within the house's years of operation. Every verified record links to the catalogue entries
    it was checked against.
  </p>
  <p>
    Titles marked <span class="badge">unverified</span> have not yet been matched: they may come
    from a crowd-sourced record without a library catalogue source, or from a published list that
    no catalogue record has confirmed.
  </p>

  <h2>Sources</h2>
  <p>
    Much of the catalogue is imported from <a href="https://openlibrary.org">Open Library</a>,
    which aggregates records from libraries worldwide. It is checked and extended against national
    and research library catalogues:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>the <a href="https://www.loc.gov/">Library of Congress</a>;</li>
    <li>the <a href="https://www.dnb.de/">German National Library</a> and the <a href="https://catalogue.bnf.fr/">Bibliothèque nationale de France</a>;</li>
    <li><a href="https://www.sudoc.abes.fr/">SUDOC</a>, the union catalogue of French university libraries, and <a href="https://swisscovery.slsp.ch/">swisscovery</a> in Switzerland;</li>
    <li>the German union catalogues <a href="https://kxp.k10plus.de/">K10plus</a> and <a href="https://www.gateway-bayern.de/">B3Kat</a>, with the Berlin and Bavarian State Libraries' East European and Chinese collections;</li>
    <li>the <a href="https://www.bn.org.pl/">National Library of Poland</a> and Spain's <a href="https://www.bne.es/">Biblioteca Nacional</a>;</li>
    <li>the <a href="https://ndlsearch.ndl.go.jp/">National Diet Library</a> and <a href="https://cir.nii.ac.jp/">CiNii</a> in Japan;</li>
    <li><a href="https://www.hathitrust.org/">HathiTrust</a>, the shared digital library of research libraries, whose records and open scans are both used;</li>
    <li>the <a href="https://archive.org/">Internet Archive</a> and <a href="https://www.bannedthought.net/">BannedThought.net</a>, whose scans are linked where a title can be read online.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Author pages draw on Wikipedia and Wikidata for biographical details. Errors in any of these
    records can carry over; titles, languages and spellings are normalised on import, but some
    mistakes will remain.
  </p>

  <h2 id="licence">Reusing the catalogue</h2>
  <p>
    Bibliotheca Marxista is free to use, copy and build on, under three licences:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      the <strong>catalogue's data</strong> (publishers, titles, author records and the downloads) under the <a href="https://opendatacommons.org/licenses/odbl/1-0/">Open Database License (ODbL) 1.0</a>: credit Bibliotheca
      Marxista, and share a database you make from it under the same licence;
    </li>
    <li>
      its <strong>texts</strong> (the histories of the houses, the authors' biographies and these pages) under <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/">Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0</a>;
    </li>
    <li>
      its <strong>code</strong> under the <a href="https://www.gnu.org/licenses/agpl-3.0.html">GNU Affero General Public
      License 3.0</a>: a modified version run as a public site must publish its changes.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The records come from the sources listed above. Most release their catalogue data into the public domain
    (Open Library, Wikidata, the Library of Congress, the German National Library, K10plus, B3Kat, the Biblioteca
    Nacional de España and the National Library of Poland), and swisscovery's are free for general use. Others are
    reused on condition that they are credited:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>records of the Bibliothèque nationale de France and of ABES (SUDOC), under the French government's <a href="https://www.etalab.gouv.fr/licence-ouverte-open-licence/">Licence Ouverte</a>;</li>
    <li>CiNii Books (National Institute of Informatics), whose NACSIS-CAT records are open data under <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">CC BY 4.0</a>;</li>
    <li>the National Diet Library, under its <a href="https://ndl.go.jp/en/use/metadata/index.html">terms for bibliographic
      data</a>, compatible with CC BY 4.0;</li>
    <li>HathiTrust, whose records include data from OCLC's WorldCat.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Scans and texts held elsewhere are linked, not copied, and remain under their own terms.
  </p>

  <h2 id="download">Download the data</h2>
  <p>
    The catalogue — publishers, author pages and titles, with their sources — can be downloaded as
    JSON for research, citation managers or other projects: the whole of it, or one publisher's
    backlist. The file is put together from the catalogue as it downloads, so it is always current.
  </p>
  <form class="data-download" action={`${API}/export`} method="get">
    <label>
      <span>What to download</span>
      <select name="pub">
        <option value="">The whole catalogue</option>
        {sortedPublishers.map((p) => <option value={p.id}>{p.data.name}</option>)}
      </select>
    </label>
    <button type="submit" class="button">Download (JSON)</button>
    <p class="meta" data-status>
      {n(publishers.length)} publishers, {n(authors.length)} authors and {n(titles.length)} titles.
      The whole catalogue comes to several hundred megabytes; a single publisher is quick.
    </p>
  </form>
</Base>
